    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Tenino grew into a boomtown in the late 19th century thanks to its sandstone supply exported to Seattle and San Francisco after fires.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.experienceolympia.com/our-communities/tenino/"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s name traces back to the steamboat Tenino, itself named after the Tenino native band of the Warm Springs tribes.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tenino%2C_Washington"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1872 the Northern Pacific Railroad built a station on Hodgden's farm, naming the stop Tenino and sparking the town’s founding.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        8,
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2",
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://teninoacc.org/tenino-history/"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Early settlers Stephen Hodgden arrived from Maine during the Gold Rush, turning his farm into Hodgden's Station, a stagecoach stop to Olympia.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://teninoacc.org/tenino-history/"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Logging and farming dominated Tenino’s economy, making it a trading center for south‑central Thurston County in the early 20th century.",
